Hexagon host: the per-token host timers, the graph cache by uid, and the packed batch cache

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9, with the patches hexagon-fusion 0001 thru 0004, hexagon-vit 0001
and llama-tied-head 0001 applied. The hunks of ggml-hexagon.cpp assume that state.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-backend.cpp               the split timers, and the skip of a split with no computable node
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp  the host timers of a session, the graph cache by uid, the packed batch cache

How to apply, from the llama.cpp root:

    git apply patches/hexagon-host/0001-hexagon-host-timers-and-batch-cache.patch
    cmake --build build-snapdragon -j

Only the host libraries change: libggml-base.so and libggml-hexagon.so (build-snapdragon/bin). The DSP
library stays as it is. The descriptors that the DSP reads do not change.

Switches (environment variables):

    LLAMA_HOSTPROF=1            print one line for each token with the host times of the session
    GGML_HEXAGON_BATCHCACHE=N   0 = pack the ops of each graph_compute call (the previous behavior),
                                1 = replay the packed batch of a repeated graph (the preset value),
                                2 = replay, and compare each replay with a fresh packing
    GGML_HEXAGON_GRAPHCACHE=N   the number of graphs that a session keeps (the preset value is 8)
    GGML_SCHED_SKIP_NOCOMPUTE_SPLITS=N  1 = skip a split with no computable node (the preset value),
                                0 = compute each split

The skip of a split with no computable node

ggml_build_forward_select puts the nodes of the two branches in the graph, and gives the flag
GGML_TENSOR_FLAG_COMPUTE to the nodes of the branch that it selects only. The CPU backend skips a node
without that flag (ggml-cpu.c), but the scheduler still makes a split for such a node, copies the inputs
of the split, and calls the backend. The token embedding of llm_graph_context::build_inp_embd is such a
branch: with the embedding rows as the input (patch 0002), the GET_ROWS of the tokens stays in the graph
without the flag, and its split on the CPU stays. The scheduler now skips a split when no node of it
carries the flag: the inputs of that split are not read, thus their copies are not necessary. The result
of the graph does not change, because a backend must not compute such a node.

The host timers

With LLAMA_HOSTPROF=1 the session prints, at each synchronize call that follows a batch:

    hostprof: HTP0 graphs 1 ops 513 hits 1 replays 1 verified 0 batches 1 | pack 40 submit 210 wait 25100
              pop 0 get 95 (993280 B) set 12 (20480 B) | turnaround 3900 us

The fields are microseconds: pack is the time in graph_compute (the op packing, or the replay check),
submit is the time in flush_batch (the copy of the descriptors into the queue block and the queue write),
wait is the time in dspqueue_read, pop is the time of the profile dump of the responses, get and set are
the tensor copies, and turnaround is the time from the last response of the previous token to the first
submit of this token. The scheduler prints one line for each compute call:

    hostprof: sched splits 2 [CPU nodes 1 inputs 0 copy 0 compute 180] [HTP0 nodes 898 inputs 8 copy 35 compute 900] us

The op node cache by uid

The session kept the op nodes of one graph (cached_uid). A draft context and a target context alternate
two graphs on the same session, thus each call rebuilt the nodes. The session now keeps a map from the
graph uid to the op nodes and the packed batch, with GGML_HEXAGON_GRAPHCACHE entries. When the map is
full, the graph that ran the longest time ago goes.

The packed batch cache

The descriptors of a batch (the buffers, the tensors and the ops) depend on four inputs: the graph (its
uid names the topology, the shapes and the types), the data pointer of each tensor, the op params of each
node, and the slot index of each fused state op of a gated delta net layer. When a graph_compute call
packs one graph into one batch, and nothing else is in that batch, the session keeps the descriptors with
the list of the tensors and the nodes they came from. When the same graph runs again, the session compares
the data pointer of each referenced tensor and the op params of each node with the kept values, reads the
slot index of each fused state op again, and loads the kept descriptors into the batch. The flush of the
batch happens where it happened before (the next synchronize or get-tensor call), thus the batch
boundaries do not change. The DSP reads the descriptors of each batch from the queue block as before,
thus it needs no change and no other flush: the DSP cleans its cache at the start and at the end of each
batch, and the host writes the input tensors before the submit as before.

A replay is not possible, and the session packs the graph again, when: a data pointer or an op param
differs, a slot index is not a row of the state table (the unfused chain must run), the batch holds ops
of a different call (a copy between sessions), the graph did not fit in one batch, or an op has a dummy
node with a per-call value (a fence sequence).

GGML_HEXAGON_BATCHCACHE=2 packs the graph a second time into a separate batch after each replay and
compares the three descriptor arrays byte for byte. A difference is an error line that names the first
differing entry, and the fresh packing runs. The count of the confirmed replays is the "verified" field of
the hostprof line.

The profile dump (GGML_HEXAGON_PROFILE) needs the op nodes of a batch. The queue kept a copy of the whole
op node array of the batch (1280 nodes) for each push. It now keeps a shared pointer to the nodes of the
batch, and only when the profile is on.
